       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="what-are-canada-timezones" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="What Are Canada's Time Zones">
    <h2>1. What Are Canada's Time Zones?</h2>
    <p>
      Canada is the second-largest country in the world by land area, stretching over 9,306 kilometers from Cape Spear in Newfoundland to the boundary of Alaska. 
      This vast geographic expanse covers nearly 88 degrees of longitude. Because of this massive horizontal footprint, a single unified clock is impossible. 
      Canada utilizes six primary standard time zones to align community schedules with the daily path of the sun.
    </p>
    
    <div class="tip-box">
      <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Under the Canadian Constitution, timekeeping is a provincial and territorial jurisdiction. 
      This means local municipal councils and provincial legislative assemblies—not the federal government in Ottawa—have the legal power to decide timezone boundaries and seasonal Daylight Saving Time rules.
    </div>

    <p>
      This decentralization explains why boundaries do not always align with provincial borders. 
      For example, northwestern Ontario uses Central Time to stay in sync with Manitoba, while the eastern portion of the province uses Eastern Time. 
      Additionally, the National Research Council (NRC) in Ottawa maintains Canada's official atomic clocks, but it has no legislative authority to dictate which offsets local communities must observe. 
      Our calculations relative to <a href="/gmt-vs-utc/">Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)</a> and <a href="/what-is-gmt/">Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</a> are officially referenced against these standards.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 1: Canada Time Zone Map -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
