  <section id="province-territory-guide" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Province & Territory Time Zone Guide">
    <h2>4. Province &amp; Territory Time Zone Guide</h2>
    <p>
      Because time rules are governed locally, it is critical to look at the exact boundaries for each of Canada's ten provinces and three territories:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>British Columbia:</strong> The vast majority of the province observes permanent Pacific Daylight Time (PDT, UTC-7) year-round. However, the Peace River region (including Dawson Creek) and Creston observe Mountain Standard Time (MST, UTC-7) year-round. The East Kootenay region observes Mountain Time with standard DST shifts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Alberta:</strong> Adopted permanent Mountain Daylight Time (MDT, UTC-6) in 2026, eliminating clock changes. The only exception is Lloydminster, which observes Alberta's standard Mountain Time rules.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Saskatchewan:</strong> Observes Central Standard Time (CST, UTC-6) year-round. The border town of Lloydminster is the only major exception, aligning with Alberta's Mountain Time to prevent municipal scheduling division.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Manitoba:</strong> Observes Central Time (CST/CDT) and participates in seasonal Daylight Saving Time shifts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ontario:</strong> The majority of the province east of 90° W longitude observes Eastern Time (EST/EDT) with seasonal DST changes. Northwestern Ontario (including Kenora and Rainy River) uses Central Time (CST/CDT).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Quebec:</strong> Most of the province observes Eastern Time (EST/EDT). The Côte-Nord region east of the 63rd meridian W and Blanc-Sablon observe Atlantic Standard Time (AST, UTC-4) year-round, while the Magdalen Islands use Atlantic Time with seasonal changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>New Brunswick:</strong> Observes Atlantic Time (AST/ADT) with standard seasonal DST changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nova Scotia:</strong> Observes Atlantic Time (AST/ADT) with standard seasonal DST changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Prince Edward Island:</strong> Observes Atlantic Time (AST/ADT) with standard seasonal DST changes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>New Newfoundland and Labrador:</strong> The island of Newfoundland and the southeastern tip of Labrador observe Newfoundland Time (NST/NDT) with seasonal DST. The remainder of Labrador observes Atlantic Time (AST/ADT).
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Yukon:</strong> Observes Mountain Standard Time (MST, UTC-7) year-round, which is referred to locally as Yukon Standard Time.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Northwest Territories:</strong> Observes Mountain Time (MST/MDT) and participates in seasonal DST shifts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Nunavut:</strong> Spans three time zones: Mountain Time (Kitikmeot region), Central Time (Kivalliq region), and Eastern Time (Qikiqtaaluk region). Coral Harbour remains on EST (UTC-5) year-round.
      </li>
    </ul>

