  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 5: PROVINCES WITH MULTIPLE TIME ZONES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="provinces-multiple-zones" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Provinces and Territories with Multiple Time Zones">
    <h2>5. Provinces and Territories with Multiple Time Zones</h2>
    <p>
      Several Canadian provinces and territories span multiple time zones due to their massive size or local trade patterns. 
      This requires residents and businesses in those regions to pay close attention to county and municipal boundaries.
    </p>

    <div class="warning-box">
      <strong>Warning:</strong> Never assume that a province uses a single time zone. 
      For example, if you are traveling west from Thunder Bay to Kenora in Ontario, you will cross the 90° W timezone boundary and lose one hour, transitioning from Eastern Time to Central Time.
    </div>

    <h3>Ontario's Division</h3>
    <p>
      Ontario is divided by the 90th meridian west. 
      Areas east of this line (including Toronto, Ottawa, and Thunder Bay) observe Eastern Time. 
      Areas west of this line (including Kenora, Rainy River, and Dryden) observe Central Time, keeping them synchronized with neighboring Manitoba, which is their primary trading partner.
    </p>

    <h3>British Columbia's Regional Offsets</h3>
    <p>
      Although British Columbia uses Pacific Time as its primary standard, eastern sections of the province are aligned with Mountain Time. 
      The Peace River region (including Dawson Creek) remains on Mountain Standard Time (UTC-7) year-round. 
      This aligns them with Pacific Daylight Time in the summer and Alberta's Mountain Time in the winter. 
      The town of Creston also observes MST year-round, while the East Kootenay region (including Cranbrook) follows standard Mountain Time with DST shifts.
    </p>

    <h3>Quebec's Atlantic Strip</h3>
    <p>
      While the vast majority of Quebec uses Eastern Time, the Côte-Nord region east of the 63rd meridian W (including Blanc-Sablon and the Magdalen Islands) uses Atlantic Time. 
      Blanc-Sablon remains on Atlantic Standard Time (UTC-4) year-round, meaning it does not observe seasonal clock changes.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 6: DAYLIGHT SAVING TIME IN CANADA
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="dst-in-canada" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Daylight Saving Time in Canada">
    <h2>6. Daylight Saving Time (DST) in Canada</h2>
    <p>
      Daylight Saving Time (DST) was first introduced in Canada during World War I to conserve coal and electricity. 
      Today, in the areas that observe seasonal shifts, DST begins on the second Sunday of March and ends on the first Sunday of November. 
      On the second Sunday of March at 2:00 AM, clocks "spring forward" one hour to 3:00 AM. 
      On the first Sunday of November at 2:00 AM, clocks "fall back" one hour to 1:00 AM. 
      To understand this concept further, read our guide on <a href="/daylight-saving-time-explained/">Daylight Saving Time Explained</a>.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, the practice of changing clocks has faced growing public opposition. 
      In 2020, Yukon eliminated seasonal clock changes. 
      In 2026, British Columbia and Alberta followed suit, adopting permanent Pacific Daylight Time (PDT, UTC-7) and permanent Mountain Daylight Time (MDT, UTC-6) respectively. 
      This has created a new timezone landscape in Western Canada. 
      You can read a detailed <a href="/est-vs-edt/">EST vs EDT comparison</a> to see how standard and daylight timings compare globally.
    </p>

