  <section id="map-explained" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Canada Time Zone Map Explained">
    <h2>7. Canada Time Zone Map Explained</h2>
    <p>
      A close examination of the Canadian timezone map reveals how boundaries are drawn to keep regional economic centers in sync. 
      Rather than drawing straight vertical lines, boundaries curve around municipal borders to prevent local commercial division.
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, the boundary between Central and Eastern Time in Ontario runs just west of Shebandowan and Upsala, allowing major regional forestry and mining communities to remain on the same time as Thunder Bay. 
      Similarly, in Nunavut, timezone boundaries are aligned with the borders of the territory's three administrative regions (Kitikmeot, Kivalliq, and Qikiqtaaluk) to prevent administrative confusion.
    </p>

    <div class="tip-box">
      <strong>Travel Tip:</strong> If you are planning a road trip across Canada via the Trans-Canada Highway, you will cross five timezone boundaries. 
      Be prepared to adjust your watch and dashboard clocks when crossing between British Columbia, Alberta, Saskatchewan, Manitoba, Ontario, Quebec, New Brunswick, and Newfoundland.
    </div>
  </section>

