  <section id="business-across-zones" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Business Operations Across Canadian Time Zones">
    <h2>9. Business Operations Across Canadian Time Zones</h2>
    <p>
      Operating a business across a country that spans six time zones requires robust scheduling systems, clear communication policies, and flexible hours for remote teams. 
      Key industry sectors face distinct operational challenges:
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 7: Business Meeting Across Canada Illustration -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/business-meeting-canada.webp" 
        alt="Business Meeting Across Canada Illustration showing scheduling overlap" 
        title="Business Meeting Across Canada Illustration" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="1000" 
        height="600" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 7: Business Meeting Coordination Across Canada.</strong> Illustration showing the optimal business meeting overlap windows for teams distributed across Vancouver, Calgary, Toronto, and Halifax.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Remote Work:</strong> Organizations with employees in Vancouver and Halifax must manage a 4-hour time difference. 
        To prevent meeting fatigue, companies often establish core collaboration hours (e.g., 12:00 PM to 4:00 PM Eastern Time), allowing all team members to participate during their standard working hours.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Customer Support:</strong> National service providers must structure support shifts to cover the "8-to-8" window. 
        A support center operating from 8:00 AM Eastern Time in Toronto will only cover customers up to 5:00 PM Pacific Time in Vancouver. 
        Staffing support teams across multiple regional hubs is the standard solution.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Banking and Financial Markets:</strong> The Toronto Stock Exchange (TSX) sets the national pace. 
        Banking transactions and clearing services must align with Eastern Time hours. 
        West Coast commercial clients must finalize their wire transfers and major transactions before the early afternoon cut-off times.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Healthcare and Logistics:</strong> National telemedicine networks and courier services (like Canada Post or Purolator) use Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) or Eastern Time as their core logistics standard to track package flows and coordinate overnight air freight schedules.
      </li>
    </ul>

