  <section id="programming-technology" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Programming & Technology Guidelines">
    <h2>10. Programming &amp; Technology Guidelines</h2>
    <p>
      In software development, managing Canadian time zones requires strict adherence to international standards to prevent scheduling bugs, data corruption, and logging errors. 
      This is especially critical due to the regional exceptions and permanent DST adoptions in BC and Alberta.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 9: Programming & UTC Workflow -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/programming-utc-workflow.webp" 
        alt="Programming and UTC Timezone Database Workflow diagram" 
        title="Programming & UTC Workflow" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="1000" 
        height="500" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 9: Developer Timezone Database Workflow.</strong> Structural diagram demonstrating the best practice of storing timezone-agnostic Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) in databases and translating to local IANA zone names in user interfaces.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>1. Database Storage</h3>
    <p>
      Always store timestamps in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC). 
      Use the standard ISO 8601 format: `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`. 
      Avoid storing local times directly in database fields, as this makes queries across different provinces nearly impossible.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Use IANA Time Zone Identifiers</h3>
    <p>
      Do not hardcode offsets (like -5 or -8) in your codebase. 
      Offsets change seasonally and legislatively. Instead, use the official IANA Time Zone Database names. 
      Examples for Canada include:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>`America/Vancouver` (Pacific Time)</li>
      <li>`America/Edmonton` (Mountain Time)</li>
      <li>`America/Regina` (Saskatchewan Central Time)</li>
      <li>`America/Winnipeg` (Central Time)</li>
      <li>`America/Toronto` (Eastern Time)</li>
      <li>`America/Halifax` (Atlantic Time)</li>
      <li>`America/St_Johns` (Newfoundland Time)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. JavaScript Localization Example</h3>
    <p>
      To display a database UTC timestamp in Toronto's local time, utilize the native `Intl.DateTimeFormat` API:
    </p>

    <pre style="background: var(--panel); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--line); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text); font-family: monospace;">{`const utcDate = new Date('2026-07-15T12:00:00Z');
const formatter = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-CA', {
  timeZone: 'America/Toronto',
  dateStyle: 'medium',
  timeStyle: 'long'
});
console.log(formatter.format(utcDate)); 
// Output will render in Toronto local time (EDT)`}</pre>
  </section>

