       SECTION 11: COMMON MISTAKES TO AVOID
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="common-mistakes" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Time Zone Mistakes to Avoid">
    <h2>11. Common Time Zone Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
    <p>
      Working and traveling across Canada involves navigating several unique timezone rules. 
      Avoid these common pitfalls:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Neglecting the 30-minute Newfoundland Offset:</strong> Many automated schedulers and developers forget that Newfoundland Time uses a half-hour offset. 
        If you schedule a national meeting and assume all offsets are in whole hours, you will exclude participants in St. John's.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Assuming Saskatchewan Changes Clocks:</strong> Saskatchewan remains on Central Standard Time (CST) year-round. 
        Do not send calendar invites that automatically shift Saskatchewan participants' clocks in March or November.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Confusing Border Town Rules:</strong> Double-check municipal rules for border communities. 
        For example, Lloydminster uses Mountain Time to align with Alberta, even though it is physically located on the Saskatchewan border.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ignoring Ontario's 90° W Split:</strong> If you are coordinating with teams in northwestern Ontario, remember they are in the Central Time Zone, while the rest of the province is in the Eastern Time Zone.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

